# /etc/services - the programs init supervises.
#
# One per line. init starts each at boot and restarts it when it dies.
# Comments (#) and blank lines are ignored.
#
# How this differs from /etc/rc:
#   rc        runs once and finishes. Nothing revives what it started.
#   services  init keeps watching. If it dies, it comes back.
#
# The reason the SSH server belongs here is plain: if it died to a bug or
# to memory pressure, there would be no way left to reach the machine.
#
# Something that dies instantly gets a growing delay between restarts,
# and after 20 failures init gives up on it. That stops a busy loop.

# The SSH server.
#   -F  stay in the foreground. init can only supervise a child that
#       stays alive; going to the background looks like an instant death.
#   -R  generate a host key if there is none
#   -E  log to the console
#   -r  where the host key lives. On the data partition, so it survives
#       a reboot and clients do not see the key change.
dropbear -F -R -E -p 22 -r /data/dropbear_ed25519_host_key

# The clock keeper.
#   This board has no battery-backed clock. Pull the power and time
#   stops; power it up again and it starts at 1970. That makes file
#   timestamps nonsense and breaks every HTTPS certificate check.
#   -d  writes the time to /data/.clock every 30s and resyncs from the
#       network hourly. /etc/rc restores that value at boot.
ntp -d


# Log collector.
#   Without this, everything printed goes to the console and is gone as
#   soon as it scrolls. If the board wedges overnight there is no record
#   of what happened - which is when you most want one.
#   Collects the kernel ring buffer and /dev/log into /data/log/messages,
#   rotating at 512KB so it can never fill the card.
logd

# Watchdog.
#   The BCM2835 has a hardware countdown that reboots the board when it
#   reaches zero. The kernel driver was always in the build; nothing was
#   resetting the timer, so it was never armed and never fired.
#   Now: if the system wedges hard enough that this stops running, the
#   board reboots itself in 15 seconds instead of waiting for someone to
#   walk over and pull the power.
#   (No watchdog hardware in a VM - it exits with a clear message there.)
watchdog -t 15
